CASE = [
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> In 1848 the French artist Frederic Sorrieu prepared a series of four prints visualising a world made up of democratic and social republics. In the first print, men and women of all ages and social classes, carrying the national flags of their countries, pass the Statue of Liberty. The statue holds the torch of Enlightenment in one hand and the Charter of the Rights of Man in the other. "
   r"(i) Who was Frederic Sorrieu? (ii) What does the Statue of Liberty hold in her two hands? (iii) What is shown by people carrying their own national flags? (iv) Why was this a 'dream' and not the reality of Europe in 1848?",
   r"(i) A French artist (1). (ii) The torch of Enlightenment and the Charter of the Rights of Man (1). (iii) Each people's wish to be a free nation, forming a brotherhood of nations (1). (iv) Most of Europe was still ruled by autocratic monarchies and multi-national empires, which suppressed the demand for national and democratic rights (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> In 1834 a customs union or Zollverein was formed at the initiative of Prussia and joined by most German states. The union abolished tariff barriers and reduced the number of currencies from over thirty to two. Earlier, a merchant travelling from one German state to another had to pass many customs points and pay duties at each. Railways further linked the economy with nation-building. "
   r"(i) What was the Zollverein? (ii) Which state took the initiative to form it? (iii) By how many did it reduce the number of currencies? (iv) How did the Zollverein strengthen the feeling of nationalism?",
   r"(i) A customs union that removed tariff barriers between the German states (1). (ii) Prussia (1). (iii) From over thirty to two (1). (iv) Free movement of goods and a common currency created economic ties among Germans; railways added to this, so people began to feel economically and then politically part of one nation (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> The Balkans was a region of geographical and ethnic variation comprising modern Romania, Bulgaria, Albania, Greece, Croatia, Bosnia-Herzegovina, Slovenia, Serbia and Montenegro. Its inhabitants were broadly known as Slavs. A large part of the Balkans was under the control of the Ottoman empire, whose power was disintegrating. "
   r"(i) Which empire controlled a large part of the Balkans? (ii) By what common name were the inhabitants of the Balkans known? (iii) Name any two modern countries of the Balkans mentioned above. (iv) Why did the Balkans become a source of big power tension, and which war did this lead to?",
   r"(i) The Ottoman empire (1). (ii) Slavs (1). (iii) Any two, e.g. Serbia and Bulgaria (1). (iv) As the Ottoman empire weakened, Balkan peoples struggled for independence while European powers (Russia, Germany, England, Austro-Hungary) competed for trade, colonies and military strength and for control of the area; this led to the First World War (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

